Senior-level answer: I use an abstract class when related page objects or services need common implementation 
and state. I use an interface when multiple implementations must
 follow the same contract without being tied to a shared class hierarchy.
 
 Interview-ready answer: “I use encapsulation to protect WebDriver and page elements,
  abstraction to expose reusable operations, inheritance to share common test setup,
   and polymorphism to support different implementations behind the same interface.”

A. Encapsulation

Encapsulation keeps data and its implementation details inside a class and exposes controlled operations.
    
